ch is soil water  air plants animals and then we added  energy and then also a human  considerations so those are the type of  benefit categories that we look at when  we're comparing things and we do this  not only for our farm but we do this  when we're looking at policy analysis as  well it's consistent and the costs of  course are the consistent over the  beautiful economic cost of land labor  capital management English and in this  case but all farming on-farm benefits  will differ it just depends on the  viewpoint if you're looking from a  policy viewpoint you're looking at the  off farm benefits and for to be talking  to a producer about implementing  practices of course we're looking at  what is the impact of them so these are  some templates that we have developed I  believe Paul maybe can help me or maybe  some contributions here is around 130  practices or so that we don't have  standards and specifications for so a  counterpart of mine took those 130  practices templates and they basically  take the categories that I showed you  before and talked about for each of  these practices  what could the potential positive  effects and negative effects be for a  producer if they were to implement that  practice and that's helpful because when  a conservation planner goes on a farm  and they want to chocolate  laundry list to start with and there  might be some additional things that are  particularly on that farm but it's a  really good laundry list that you can go  through and say okay this doesn't apply  here but something else does and it  helps them categorize the benefits in  the costs so I gave an example here of  cover cropping we call those tea charts  by the way  okay so when you're looking at um  economics and what we teach our  conservation plan is we're really  looking at the change from before to  after we don't have to look at  everything we don't have to look at  enterprise budgets and all that unless  we're um really winnowing down to  something 