t you can come up with determine what  the average cost per acre per year is I  won't put into detail but it can be done  um you can reduce the cost by decreasing  fertilizer reducing tillage passes  reducing fuel and labor there's other  positive effects um improve soil and  water quality and you may have some sort  of habitat improvement for wildlife and  then the negative affects what they do  is they take something like a purchased  milled thrill and we amortize it over a  time period and that when you do all  that then you can come up with $1 for  acre per year positive impacts and  negative impact so if you look  let me see here what the wrong button  didn't I  okay there you go down here that's the  sum of all the benefits on $60 per acre  per year in this example and then the  negative affects the total is $22 per  acre per year and so the net benefits  would be thirty-eight dollars per acre  three  so another thing that we do is we take  all this information and a lot of  producers might say well this all sounds  well and good but how does this really  work I'd like to talk to people or I'd  like to see some examples of real life  experiences so that's what we're doing  the case studies and the benefit is that  usually we use the partial budget  technique  it does give real-world examples we work  with the producers to develop them um a  good case study is not just a shining  example it also shows the hardships that  they've gone through to get to where  they are and that's really important  because we don't want to paint a rosy  picture all the time but they're gonna  have trials and trips when they do when  they change what they're doing um but  you want to make sure that it's  relatable so whoever your your audience  is so you pick a producer that's either  is respected or it relates they've got  very similar circumstances to the  audience that you're trying to provide  this basically for and um one thing I  want to say is when you look at these  case studies and there's a lot of them  out there you want to look read it with  a critical eye because different people  have different agendas and there's some  times when they say all this looks  really really good you've really got to  get into the numbers to see if it's if  they're accurate or not sometimes they  inflate benefits or they decrease costs  and things like that so make sure that  when you're looking at things that  you're really reading it with a critical  eye and ask no questions  okay okay so I'm going to the two  examples that I'm going to hand out to  people if you're interested or angel  Rose dairy and in this case they did um  both of these are in central New York  around Ontario northern Watertown area I  believe what is in social New York  somewhere right yeah  okay and that's the angel Rose dairy  what they have been doing no-till and  cover crop and then 350 acres and the  total net benefits um it's over eight  thousand dollars or twenty four dollars  per acre per year and that you can read  more details about this I'll hand it out  in these paste on the pronounce the  other thing I want to say about the case  studies is we try to make them two pages  to hand out they're meant to go to field  days and things like that so that people  can very quickly fitmom look at it and  it's not a whole bunch of papers they  don't have to delve into a lot of  research or whatever they can cut to the  chase right away um it's fine now it  wasn't interesting that you're up to  eight eight hundred cows now I think  ours was uh six six seventy or somewhere  around there and we did the case study  so it's great that you're able to  increase production on your farm um but  anyway uh if there was to 2,200 acres at  that point which was two years ago I  believe had told them that benefits of  over seventy eight thousand dollars for  sixty two dollars per acre per year  you  so some things to remember when you're  doing partial budgeting or looking at a  button is when you adopt a conservation  system you are looking at a long term  investment especially with soil health  it's going to take time it depends on  how degraded your soil is you're gonna  see actually I think they say that  you'll see more benefits thicker and  more degraded soil then very improve  their economic from also result in  economic benefits um  and an example is the the reduced yield  variability so if you're reducing risk  as soil health increases you're not  going to get them as much of a wild  swing in yields that you might have had  before and sometimes that's hard to  quantify and it's something to be  discussed and in order to realize the  greatest benefits for the system you  have to figure out the system that works  best on your operation given your  challenges and goals and I think that's  it  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu

